{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NK9Fwfcir4ZCi7CJDuEy5J","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have the full picture. Let me write the comprehensive report:\n\n## WHIR Opening Prover Trace Analysis\n\n**Entry Point:** `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:62` - `prove_whir_opening_gpu()`\n\n### Call Chain:\n\n1. **`prove_whir_opening_gpu()`** (whir.rs:62-531)\n   - Takes `stacked_per_commit: Vec<StackedPcsData2>` which contains all commitments to be opened\n   - At line 97: `num_commits = stacked_per_commit.len()` - loops **per commitment** not per AIR\n   - Each commitment can have multiple AIRs if they share cached traces\n\n2. **`prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu()`** (stacked_reduction.rs:187+)\n   - Called before WHIR at gpu_backend.rs:167\n   - Constructs `stacked_per_commit` at stacked_reduction.rs:306-319\n   - **Per-AIR loop structure** (line 307): `for (air_idx, air_ctx) in ctx.per_trace.iter()`\n   - For each AIR: adds preprocessed_data (line 308-310) + cached_mains (line 311)\n   - Each entry creates separate `StackedPcsData2` pushed to vector (line 317)\n\n### CUDA Kernels Launched in WHIR:\n\n| Kernel | File | Line | Scales With |\n|--------|------|------|------------|\n| `whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel` | whir.cu:24 | 236 | **Cell count** (height × total_width), per-packet loop in kernel |\n| `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round_kernel` | whir.cu:83 | 262 | **Cell count** (height/2 iterations) |\n| `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments_kernel` | whir.cu:141 | 289 | **Cell count** (height/2 iterations) |\n| `w_moments_accumulate_kernel` | whir.cu:196 | 305 | **Cell count** (height iterations) × num_queries |\n\n### Key Scaling Analysis:\n\n**What scales with AIR count (623 AIRs @ APC300):**\n1. **`stacked_per_commit` construction** (stacked_reduction.rs:307-319): Loop over `per_trace` AIRs, each creates separate commitment entry\n2. **Batching packet creation** (whir.rs:103-122): Creates packet per trace per committed data (`for stacked in &stacked_per_commit` → `for trace, idx in zip`)\n3. At APC300 with 623 AIRs: ~620+ additional entries in `stacked_per_commit` beyond common_main\n\n**What scales with cell count (constant ~2.8M cells):**\n1. **`whir_algebraic_batch_traces_kernel`** (whir.cu:24-74): \n   - Iterates over `num_packets` in loop at line 40\n   - Each packet's trace is indexed by stacked row/col (lines 56-68)\n   - Launch: line 236 uses `kernel_launch_params(stacked_height)` - grid/block based on **height only**\n   - GPU work: O(stacked_height) threads × per-packet overhead\n   \n2. **Sumcheck rounds** (lines 202-271):\n   - `k_whir` sumcheck rounds per WHIR round\n   - Each calls `whir_sumcheck_coeff_moments_round` (line 224)\n   - Then `whir_fold_coeffs_and_moments` (line 255)\n   - **CPU-side**: D2H transfer of 2 values (line 237), CPU transcript ops (line 239)\n\n3. **w_moments_accumulate** (line 500-510):\n   - Kernel launched **per WHIR round** only (non-last rounds)\n   - CPU-side computation of z-powers for each query (lines 482-496)\n   - Per-AIR overhead: O(log_height) per query power computation (vector allocations at 482, 489)\n\n### Per-AIR Overhead Found:\n\n**Initial round (line 375-436):**\n- `batch_query_merkle_proofs()` called once per commit (line 400)\n- `batch_open_rows()` called once per commit (line 419)\n- At 623 AIRs: 623+ separate Merkle operations\n- Per-commit loop at line 355: `for (commit_idx, stacked) in stacked_per_commit.iter()`\n- Initial_round_opened_rows vector indexed by commit at line 427\n\n**All rounds (line 445-464):**\n- Query sampling at line 366-370: per-round, per-query\n- Merkle operations at line 446-463: **per-commit per round** when whir_round > 0\n\n### Batching Opportunity:\n\nThe **algebraic batching at line 128** (`whir_algebraic_batch_traces`) already batches all AIRs into one kernel call, but:\n- Packet creation is per-trace (lines 105-119): O(num_commits) CPU overhead\n- Packet copying to device at line 122: H2D transfer scales with num_commits\n- At 623 AIRs: ~620+ extra packets, each with device pointer traversal\n\n### Time Attribution:\n\nAt APC 300 (130ms total WHIR across 2 segments = 65ms/segment):\n- **~10-20ms** likely in packet assembly, H2D transfers, per-commit Merkle proofs (623 AIRs)\n- **~45-55ms** in CUDA kernels (sumcheck, fold, moments - scales with cell count only)\n\nAt APC 0 (44ms/segment): 99 AIRs reduces overhead by ~620 packets + Merkle operations, leaving primarily CUDA kernel work.\n\n**Optimization target:** Batch packet creation and Merkle proof queries by commit group rather than individual AIR traces."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb5d1119-b0b0-4361-9117-64026afc483a","uuid":"ab20c077-c1c6-4d03-8766-65084782b3a2","timestamp":"2026-04-15T08:05:54.158Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm optimizing a GPU STARK prover. I need to understand the WHIR opening prover code path. WHIR is the polynomial opening proof scheme used in the Openings phase. At APC 300 (623 AIRs), WHIR takes 130ms across 2 segments (~65ms per segment). At APC 0 (99 AIRs), it's ~44ms per segment.\n\nThe codebase is at /home/georg/stark-backend. The CUDA backend is in crates/cuda-backend.\n\nPlease:\n1. Find the WHIR prover entry point (search for \"whir\" in the cuda-backend and stark-backend crates)\n2. Trace the full call chain for the WHIR opening prover\n3. Identify what CUDA kernels are launched during WHIR (search for kernel names like whir_algebraic, whir_fold, whir_sumcheck, w_moments)\n4. Understand what scales with the number of AIR instances vs what scales with cell count\n5. Look for per-AIR loops or overhead that could be batched\n\nReport what you find in detail: file paths, line numbers, what each function does, and where time is likely spent. Under 500 words.","agentId":"a08c3eecc79bdca5a","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! Now I have the fu