ysis, the allocations cluster at either end: small ones under 16 MiB already use cudaMallocAsync, and large ones over 100 MiB use VPMM. The exact distribution in the 16-48 MiB range is unclear without profiling. The VPMM task report notes that common benchmark sizes like 48, 144, and 192 MiB are exact multiples of 16 MiB with no waste, and all of these exceed 32 MiB anyway, so increasing the page size to 32 MiB would affect how these larger allocations are handled.\n\nLooking at the specific impact: 48 MiB would round up to 64 MiB with 16 MiB of waste, 144 MiB to 160 MiB, and 192 MiB stays exact. The waste from 48 MiB allocations could accumulate across multiple instances, and since all the common sizes are above the threshold, none would shift to cudaMallocAsync. I should check whether the GKR pre-allocated buffers per thread fall into the 16-32 MiB range that might be affected.\n\nFrom the prealloc-gkr-input-buffers report, each worker thread gets a set of max-sized buffers including intermediates, public values, partition pointers, and temporaries. For APC 300, each buffer is around 40 MiB per thread, and with 8 threads that's 320 MiB total of pre-allocated GKR buffers that persist for the duration and route through the memory manager. Since these 40 MiB buffers exceed the 16 MiB threshold, they'd still go through VPMM.\n\nIncreasing the page size to 32 MiB wouldn't meaningfully help here since the buffers are already larger than that boundary. The VPMM agent's point stands—the 16 MiB threshold was the real sweet spot for optimization.\n\nLet me explore a different angle: looking at how APC 0 and APC 300 compare at the segment level. APC 0 has 5 segments with roughly 380M cells each totaling 1.9B cells, while APC 300 has 2 segments with roughly 405M cells each totaling 811M cells. The per-segment cell count is nearly identical between them, so the difference is just that APC 300 processes fewer segments overall.\n\nPer-segment timing tells an interesting story th