s own polynomial evaluation and folding in the MLE sumcheck, so 27x more columns creates significant overhead. This is getting into the weeds though—let me step back and systematically generate my top ideas, then pick the most promising one.\n\nI should dig into the nsight kernel data to see what fraction of WHIR time is actually GPU kernels versus CPU overhead and synchronization costs.\n\nLooking at the kernel breakdown, the algebraic batch traces kernel takes 18.3ms across 6 calls, while the sumcheck coefficient and folding kernels are much faster at around 34-41 microseconds each. The moments accumulation kernel runs at about 471 microseconds per instance. Adding these up, the GPU kernels account for roughly 31ms of the 130ms WHIR wall-clock time, leaving about 99ms unaccounted for—that's likely CPU work, synchronization, memory transfers, and NTT operations that happen within WHIR itself.\n\nNow I'm looking at the NTT-related kernels to understand their contribution... The CPU overhead per segment includes packet creation, H2D uploads for hundreds of AIRs, Merkle proof operations, and challenge sampling—if I can reduce this by half through batching, that's roughly 14ms per segment saved. But I should reconsider whether all this overhead is actually CPU-bound or if some of it involves GPU kernel launches like the algebraic batch operation that happens during the initial round and sumcheck phases.\n\nLooking at the sumcheck rounds more carefully, there's a pattern of GPU kernels for coefficient moments and folding, D2H transfers pulling values back, CPU transcript hashing, and NTT operations—and with roughly 5-6 rounds per commit across multiple commits, I'm seeing around 80 kernel instances total. I'm spending too much time dissecting the WHIR breakdown though; I should pivot to what actually seems most promising to optimize.\n\nLet me weigh my real options here: WHIR batching could save 20-40ms but has moderate complexity, VPMM tuning is hitting diminishing ret