{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H4LobzZWmmHuPa1H5JK4aZ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information. Let me compile my findings.\n\n## Summary of STARK WHIR Prover Scaling with AIR Count\n\n**WHIR Polynomial Construction:**\n\nAt `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:100-128`, the WHIR polynomial is constructed as:\n- **Height**: Fixed at `stacked_per_commit[0].layout().height()` (line 81), determined by the stacked layout and parameter `l_skip + n_stack`\n- **Width (in terms of polynomial degree)**: Derived from `total_stacked_width` (line 104-118), which **sums the widths** of all stacked layouts: `total_stacked_width += layout.width()` per commitment\n\nThe WHIR polynomial (`f_ple_evals`) has capacity `height * D_EF` base field elements (line 100), where D_EF=4. It's created via **algebraic batching** at line 128: `whir_algebraic_batch_traces()` takes:\n- One `BatchingTracePacket` per trace column in all AIRs\n- `mu_powers` array of length `total_stacked_width` (line 120)\n- Produces single output of size `height * D_EF` (line 125)\n\n**Scaling Pattern:**\n\n1. **Height is FIXED** across AIRs: all traces packed into `stacked_per_commit` share the same stacked height (`2^{l_skip + n_stack}`)\n2. **Width GROWS LINEARLY** with AIR count:\n   - From `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/stacked_pcs.rs:155-187`, each AIR's matrix width is summed: the stacked width accumulates all trace widths (line 187: `stacked_width = col_idx + ...`)\n   - Line 118 of whir.rs: `total_stacked_width += layout.width()` for each commitment\n\n**Merkle Tree Construction:**\n\nAt `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:70-75`, the Merkle tree is built over the **RS-coded stacked matrix**:\n- Dimensions: `(height << log_blowup) × (accumulated stacked width)` \n- Line 73: tree leaf node count = `1 << k_whir` (packet size)\n- Built from `rs_code_matrix()` which pads **height** by factor `2^log_blowup`, **width stays the same**\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe WHIR polynomial gets **WIDER** (more columns per row) as AIR count increases, not taller. The summation of `layout.width()` at line 118 accumulates all AIR widths into `total_stacked_width`, which directly affects the batching dimension (mu powers). Height is fixed. This affects WHIR time linearly per additional AIR width."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb5d1119-b0b0-4361-9117-64026afc483a","uuid":"af39f34a-7e46-41dd-8de1-3e9fba8d4ed0","timestamp":"2026-04-15T08:14:29.783Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I'm researching a STARK prover optimization. I need to understand how the WHIR prover constructs its polynomial and Merkle tree, and specifically what scales with the number of AIR instances.\n\nIn the codebase at /home/georg/stark-backend:\n\n1. In `crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs`, find the `prove_whir_opening_gpu` function. Look at:\n   - How `stacked_per_commit` entries are processed to create the WHIR polynomial\n   - What determines the height (number of rows) and width (columns per row) of the WHIR polynomial\n   - How `num_packets` and packet size relate to AIR count\n   - The `whir_algebraic_batch_traces` call and what it produces\n\n2. In `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs`, look at the `prove_stacked_opening_reduction_gpu` function:\n   - How opening claims from all AIRs are collected\n   - How the `stacked_per_commit` vector is built\n   - What the `StackedPcsData2` structure contains\n\n3. Check how the Merkle tree is built within WHIR: is it over the WHIR polynomial (batched from all AIRs), and what dimensions does it have?\n\nFocus on: does the WHIR polynomial get WIDER (more columns per row) or TALLER (more rows) or BOTH when there are more AIRs? This determines how per-AIR count affects WHIR time.\n\nReport concrete facts: variable names, line numbers, dimensions. Under 300 words.","agentId":"ad25d4a49c51f0221","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent! Now I have all the information. Let me compile my findings.\n\n## Summary of STARK WHIR Prover Scaling with AIR Count\n\n**WHIR Polynomial Construction:**\n\nAt `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/whir.rs:100-128`, the WHIR polynomial is constructed as:\n- **Height**: Fixed at `stacked_per_commit[0].layout().height()` (line 81), determined by the stacked layout and parameter `l_skip + n_stack`\n- **Width (in terms of polynomial degree)**: Derived from `total_stacked_width` (line 104-118), which **sums the widths** of all stacked layouts: `total_stacked_width += layout.width()` per commitment\n\nThe WHIR polynomial (`f_ple_evals`) has capacity `height * D_EF` base field elements (line 100), where D_EF=4. It's created via **algebraic batching** at line 128: `whir_algebraic_batch_traces()` takes:\n- One `BatchingTracePacket` per trace column in all AIRs\n- `mu_powers` array of length `total_stacked_width` (line 120)\n- Produces single output of size `height * D_EF` (line 125)\n\n**Scaling Pattern:**\n\n1. **Height is FIXED** across AIRs: all traces packed into `stacked_per_commit` share the same stacked height (`2^{l_skip + n_stack}`)\n2. **Width GROWS LINEARLY** with AIR count:\n   - From `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/stark-backend/src/prover/stacked_pcs.rs:155-187`, each AIR's matrix width is summed: the stacked width accumulates all trace widths (line 187: `stacked_width = col_idx + ...`)\n   - Line 118 of whir.rs: `total_stacked_width += layout.width()` for each commitment\n\n**Merkle Tree Construction:**\n\nAt `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_pcs.rs:70-75`, the Merkle tree is built over the **RS-coded stacked matrix**:\n- Dimensions: `(height << log_blowup) × (accumulated stacked width)` \n- Line 73: tree leaf node count = `1 << k_whir` (packet size)\n- Built from `rs_code_matrix()` which pads **height** by factor `2^log_blowup`, **width stays the same**\n\n**Conclusion:**\nThe WHIR polynomial gets **WIDER** (more columns per row) as AIR count increases, not taller. The summation of `layout.width()` at line 118 accumulates all AIR widths into `total_stacked_width`, which directly affects the batching dimension (mu powers). Height is fixed. This affects WHIR time linearly per additional AIR width."}],"totalDurationMs":41858,"totalTokens":44854,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1028,"cache_read_input_tokens":43101,"output_tokens":723,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1028},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":723,"cache_read_input_tokens":43101,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1028,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1028,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":2,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
