m":"Profiling the system"},{"content":"Research bottlenecks in source code","status":"completed","activeForm":"Researching bottlenecks in source code"},{"content":"Generate and rank optimization ideas","status":"completed","activeForm":"Generating and ranking optimization ideas"},{"content":"Write task.md with selected optimization","status":"in_progress","activeForm":"Writing task.md"},{"content":"Create plan.md with detailed implementation plan","status":"pending","activeForm":"Creating plan.md"},{"content":"Review loop with subagent until approved","status":"pending","activeForm":"Running review loop with subagent"}],"verificationNudgeNeeded":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012N2BLPr3n5XUGt14ja4q1T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gy2pGd3Es65E8bot8fvMDq","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold\n\nDecouple the VPMM allocation routing threshold from the physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter. Currently, allocations smaller than `page_size` (16 MiB) use `cudaMallocAsync` while larger ones go through the VPMM page-management system. The previous page-size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB gave a surprising −186 ms on APC 300 STARK excl trace, with ~136 ms coming from medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) bypassing VPMM overhead. By raising the routing threshold to 64 MiB (4× page_size) without changing the page size itself, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range (including per-thread GKR intermediates at ~20–40 MiB each) will also bypass VPMM, avoiding per-allocation cuMemCreate, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead while preserving the 16 MiB page granularity for truly large allocations (>64 MiB).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":385,"cache_read_input_tokens":151781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":385},"output_tokens":47,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb5d1119-b0b0-4361-9117-64026afc483a","uuid":"f72be9c7-ae63-45d1-8ff3-c7c591af8c56"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gy2pGd3Es65E8bot8fvMDq","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb5d1119-b0b0-4361-9117-64026afc483a","uuid":"e8fa409a-ef31-4af8-86ef-0a0f074e100a","timestamp":"2026-04-15T08:21:12.754Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/task.md","content":"# 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold\n\nDecouple the VPMM allocation routing threshold from the physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter. Currently, allocations smaller than `page_size` (16 MiB) use `cudaMallocAsync` while larger ones go through the VPMM page-management system. The previous page-size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB gave a surprising −186 ms on APC 300 STARK excl trace, with ~136 ms coming from medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) bypassing VPMM overhead. By raising the routing threshold to 64 MiB (4× page_size) without changing the page size itself, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range (including per-thread GKR intermediates at ~20–40 MiB each) will also bypass VPMM, avoiding per-allocation cuMemCreate, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead while preserving the 16 MiB page granularity for truly large allocations (>64 MiB).\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1,"newLines":3,"lines":["-# 2026-04-15-1000-vpmm-bulk-page-creation","+# 2026-04-15-1020-decouple-vpmm-pool-threshold"," ","-Replace the per-page `cuMemCreate + cuMemMap` loop in the VPMM's `defragment_or_create_new_pages` with a single bulk `cuMemCreate` of the total needed size, followed by per-page offset-based `cuMemMap` calls. The first segment's `rs_code_matrix` allocation triggers ~128 individual page creations (~58ms on APC 300 seg 0), where `cuMemCreate` is the dominant cost (~0.4ms per call). A single bulk allocation reduces this to ~7ms. Unlike the failed pool-warming approach (task `cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments`), this does not change the pool's free-region state or VA layout — it only changes how physical pages are created, leaving all downstream memory access patterns identical.","+Decouple the VPMM allocation routing threshold from the physical page size by introducing a separate `pool_threshold` parameter. Currently, allocations smaller than `page_size` (16 MiB) use `cudaMallocAsync` while larger ones go through the VPMM page-management system. The previous page-size increase from 2 MiB to 16 MiB gave a surprising −186 ms on APC 300 STARK excl trace, with ~136 ms coming from medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) bypassing VPMM overhead. By raising the routing threshold to 64 MiB (4× page_size) without changing the page size itself, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range (including per-thread GKR intermediates at ~20–40 MiB each) will also bypass VPMM, avoiding per-allocation cuMemCreate, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead while preserving the 16 MiB page granularity for truly large allocations (>64 MiB)."]}],"originalFile":"# 2026-04-15-1000-vpmm-bulk-page-creation\n\nReplace the per-page `cuMemCreate + cuMemMap` loop in the VPMM's `defragment_or_create_new_pages` with a single bulk `cuMemCreate` of the total needed size, followed by per-page offset-based `cuMemMap` calls. The first segment's `rs_code_matrix` allocation triggers ~128 individual page creations (~58ms on APC 300 seg 0), where `cuMemCreate` is the dominant cost (~0.4ms per call). A single bulk allocation reduces this to ~7ms. Unlike the failed pool-warming approach (task `cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments`), this does not change the pool's free-region state or VA layout — it only changes how physical pages are created, leaving all downstream memory access patterns identical.\n"}}
