{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NeC3MetLoSSmfKb4FbT9Ga","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Decouple VPMM Pool Threshold from Page Size\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tIntroduce a configurable `pool_threshold` parameter that determines the minimum allocation size routed to the VPMM virtual-memory system, independent of the physical `page_size`. By raising this threshold from 16 MiB (current page_size) to 64 MiB, allocations in the 16–64 MiB range will use `cudaMallocAsync` instead of the VPMM, avoiding per-allocation `cuMemCreate` calls, page tracking, and defragmentation overhead. The page_size remains at 16 MiB for large VPMM allocations.\n6\t\n7\tThis follows the pattern established by the `vpmm-bulk-page-creation` task, which showed that routing medium-sized allocations (2–16 MiB) through `cudaMallocAsync` instead of VPMM gave −136 ms on LogUp GKR + Constraints at APC 300 — far beyond the expected savings from reduced `cuMemCreate` calls alone.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Allocation routing (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:71-92`)\n12\t\n13\t```rust\n14\tfn d_malloc(&mut self, size: usize) -> Result<*mut c_void, MemoryError> {\n15\t    let mut tracked_size = size;\n16\t    let ptr = if size < self.pool.page_size {     // ← threshold = page_size\n17\t        // cudaMallocAsync path\n18\t        cudaMallocAsync(&mut ptr, size, cudaStreamPerThread);\n19\t        self.allocated_ptrs.insert(ptr, size);\n20\t        ptr\n21\t    } else {\n22\t        // VPMM path\n23\t        tracked_size = size.next_multiple_of(self.pool.page_size);\n24\t        self.pool.malloc_internal(tracked_size, stream_id)?\n25\t    };\n26\t    // ...\n27\t}\n28\t```\n29\t\n30\t### Deallocation routing (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs:104-119`)\n31\t\n32\t```rust\n33\tunsafe fn d_free(&mut self, ptr: *mut c_void) -> Result<(), MemoryError> {\n34\t    if let Some(size) = self.allocated_ptrs.remove(&nn) {\n35\t        // cudaMallocAsync path — ptr was in allocated_ptrs\n36\t        cudaFreeAsync(ptr, cudaStreamPerThread);\n37\t    } else {\n38\t        // VPMM path — ptr was in pool.malloc_regions\n39\t        self.pool.free_internal(ptr, stream_id)?;\n40\t    }\n41\t}\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tThe deallocation path is routing-agnostic: it checks `allocated_ptrs` first, falls through to VPMM. No change needed here.\n45\t\n46\t### Configuration (`crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs:35-86`)\n47\t\n48\t`VpmmConfig` has fields `page_size`, `va_size`, `initial_pages`. It loads from environment variables in `from_env()`. `VirtualMemoryPool` stores `page_size` at field `vm_pool.rs:134`.\n49\t\n50\t### Key allocations in the 16–64 MiB range\n51\t\n52\t1. **GKR intermediates** (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr_input.rs:308`): `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(max_intermediates_len)` where `max_intermediates_len = TASK_SIZE(65536) × buffer_size × sizeof(EF)(16)`. Typical buffer_size = 10–40 → 10–40 MiB per thread. With 8 threads, 8 allocations of 10–40 MiB each, all currently routed through VPMM.\n53\t\n54\t2. **Various temporary DeviceBuffers** allocated during proving (fold intermediates, evaluation buffers) that may fall in the 16–64 MiB range.\n55\t\n56\t### VPMM overhead per allocation\n57\t\n58\tFor a 40 MiB VPMM allocation with 16 MiB pages:\n59\t- `ceil(40/16) = 3` physical pages created via `cuMemCreate` (~0.4 ms each) = 1.2 ms\n60\t- `cuMemMap` + `cuMemSetAccess` per page: ~0.1 ms each = 0.3 ms\n61\t- Free-region search + zombie cleanup: ~0.01 ms\n62\t- Total: ~1.5 ms per allocation\n63\t\n64\tFor 8 GKR intermediates alone: 8 × 1.5 ms ≈ 12 ms pure allocation overhead.\n65\t\n66\tAdditionally, the previous `vpmm-bulk-page-creation` task showed that VPMM management causes secondary effects (likely TLB/VA-layout degradation for bandwidth-bound kernels), contributing −136 ms beyond the direct allocation savings at APC 300.\n67\t\n68\t## Changes\n69\t\n70\t### Change 1: Add `pool_threshold` to `VpmmConfig`\n71\t\n72\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n73\t\n74\tAdd a new field to `VpmmConfig`:\n75\t\n76\t```rust\n77\tpub struct VpmmConfig {\n78\t    pub page_size: Option<usize>,\n79\t    pub va_size: usize,\n80\t    pub initial_pages: usize,\n81\t    pub pool_threshold: Option<usize>,  // NEW\n82\t}\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\tUpdate `Default`:\n86\t```rust\n87\timpl Default for VpmmConfig {\n88\t    fn default() -> Self {\n89\t        Self {\n90\t            page_size: None,\n91\t            va_size: DEFAULT_VA_SIZE,\n92\t            initial_pages: 0,\n93\t            pool_threshold: None,  // NEW: resolved in VirtualMemoryPool::new()\n94\t        }\n95\t    }\n96\t}\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tUpdate `from_env()` to load `VPMM_POOL_THRESHOLD`:\n100\t```rust\n101\tlet pool_threshold = std::env::var(\"VPMM_POOL_THRESHOLD\").ok().map(|val| {\n102\t    let size: usize = val.parse().expect(\"VPMM_POOL_THRESHOLD must be a valid number\");\n103\t    assert!(size > 0, \"VPMM_POOL_THRESHOLD must be > 0\");\n104\t    size\n105\t});\n106\t```\n107\t\n108\t### Change 2: Store `pool_threshold` in `VirtualMemoryPool`\n109\t\n110\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n111\t\n112\tAdd field to struct `VirtualMemoryPool` (at line 134):\n113\t```rust\n114\tpub(super) pool_threshold: usize,\n115\t```\n116\t\n117\tIn `VirtualMemoryPool::new()`, after resolving `page_size` (line 178), resolve `pool_threshold`:\n118\t```rust\n119\tlet pool_threshold = match config.pool_threshold {\n120\t    Some(t) => {\n121\t        assert!(\n122\t            t >= page_size,\n123\t            \"VPMM_POOL_THRESHOLD ({}) must be >= page_size ({})\",\n124\t            t, page_size\n125\t        );\n126\t        t\n127\t    }\n128\t    // Default: 4x page_size (typically 64 MiB).\n129\t    // Allocations below this go through cudaMallocAsync.\n130\t    None => 4 * page_size,\n131\t};\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\tAdd to the `Self { ... }` initializer (line 204-219):\n135\t```rust\n136\tpool_threshold,\n137\t```\n138\t\n139\tAdd to the debug log (near line 190):\n140\t```rust\n141\ttracing::debug!(\n142\t    \"VPMM: pool_threshold={}\",\n143\t    ByteSize::b(pool_threshold as u64)\n144\t);\n145\t```\n146\t\n147\t### Change 3: Use `pool_threshold` for allocation routing\n148\t\n149\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/mod.rs`\n150\t\n151\tChange line 75 from:\n152\t```rust\n153\tlet ptr = if size < self.pool.page_size {\n154\t```\n155\tto:\n156\t```rust\n157\tlet ptr = if size < self.pool.pool_threshold {\n158\t```\n159\t\n160\tNo other changes needed. The deallocation path (`d_free`) already correctly routes based on the `allocated_ptrs` HashMap presence, which tracks all `cudaMallocAsync` allocations regardless of size.\n161\t\n162\t### Change 4: Handle VPMM-unsupported fallback (overflow guard)\n163\t\n164\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/vm_pool.rs`\n165\t\n166\tWhen VPMM is not supported by the GPU, `page_size` is set to `usize::MAX` (line 198) so all allocations use `cudaMallocAsync`. Computing `4 * usize::MAX` would overflow. Guard the default resolution in Change 2:\n167\t\n168\t```rust\n169\tNone => {\n170\t    if page_size == usize::MAX {\n171\t        usize::MAX  // VPMM not supported; all allocations use cudaMallocAsync\n172\t    } else {\n173\t        4 * page_size\n174\t    }\n175\t}\n176\t```\n177\t\n178\tThis ensures the VPMM-not-supported path continues to route all allocations through `cudaMallocAsync`.\n179\t\n180\t### Change 5: Update test callsites\n181\t\n182\t**File**: `crates/cuda-common/src/memory_manager/tests.rs`\n183\t\n184\tFour `VpmmConfig` struct-literal constructions (at lines 55-58, 117-121, 390-393, 614-617) must include the new `pool_threshold` field. Add `pool_threshold: None` to each, preserving the existing test behavior (default resolution).\n185\t\n186\t## Invariants\n187\t\n188\t1. **Allocation-deallocation consistency**: Every allocation through `cudaMallocAsync` is tracked in `allocated_ptrs`; every VPMM allocation is tracked in `pool.malloc_regions`. The `d_free` routing checks `allocated_ptrs` first. As long as the threshold is consistent between allocation and deallocation (which it is — a given pointer was allocated through exactly one path), correctness is maintained.\n189\t\n190\t2. **VPMM page alignment**: VPMM allocations are still rounded up to `page_size` multiples (line 89). This is unchanged because only allocations ≥ `pool_threshold` (≥ 64 MiB) go through VPMM. Since `pool_threshold >= page_size`, the rounding is always valid.\n191\t\n192\t3. **pool_threshold >= page_size**: Enforced by the assertion in Change 2. This ensures that VPMM never receives allocations smaller than a page.\n193\t\n194\t4. **No change to VPMM internals**: The VPMM pool's page creation, mapping, defragmentation, free-region management, and zombie cleanup are all unchanged. Only the routing decision is affected.\n195\t\n196\t5. **Proof correctness**: This optimization only changes allocation routing — the mathematical operations and data flow are identical. Any allocation size works correctly with both `cudaMallocAsync` and VPMM.\n197\t\n198\t## Measurement Plan\n199\t\n200\t1. Run `openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh` for APC {0, 100, 300} in the powdr repo.\n201\t2. Analyze with `spec.py`. Compare STARK excl trace, LogUp GKR, Round 0, MLE Rounds, Trace Commit at all APC configs.\n202\t3. Verify no regression at APC 0 (threshold: +20 ms).\n203\t4. Collect nsight profile at APC 300 for GPU kernel timeline comparison.\n204\t5. All proof configurations must complete with `--recursion` (correctness check).\n205\t\n206\tIf regression at APC 0 > 20 ms (following the pattern seen in `cache-codeword-buffer-across-segments` and `multistream-stacked-reduction-round0` where pool state changes caused GKR regressions):\n207\t- Try `pool_threshold` = 2 × page_size (32 MiB) as a more conservative alternative.\n208\t- If still regressing, try `pool_threshold` = page_size (no change, confirming the regression is from the threshold increase).\n209\t\n210\t## Rollback Criteria\n211\t\n212\t- APC 0 STARK excl trace regression > 20 ms → revert.\n213\t- APC 300 STARK excl trace improvement < 10 ms → revert (not worth the added complexity).\n214\t- Any proof correctness failure → revert.\n215\t- If the 64 MiB threshold doesn't help but 32 MiB does, keep the 32 MiB version.\n216\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01CYSyLVUWASgEojr4SuTDY5","session_id":"bb5d1119-b0b0-4361-9117-64026afc483a","uuid":"7d543303-ae2d-416c-b51d-d84e90138caa","timestamp":"2026-04-15T08:26:57.811Z"}
